WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.640
Right now, as you’re listening to this,
you are being robbed.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:11.840
A chunk of everything your hard work creates
is being stolen from you.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.290
And it’s a system called capitalism that’s
robbing you.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:20.080
Every day, when you check in to work for your
boss, you are being taken advantage of and

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.300
stolen from.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.690
You are being deprived of the full value of
what you contribute.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.269
Let me break it down.

00:00:26.269 --> 00:00:32.379
A capitalist isn’t someone in a top hat
burning library books to run a misery factory.

00:00:32.379 --> 00:00:39.350
Anyone who puts forward capital – money
– to set workers in motion becomes a capitalist,

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:41.469
that is to say, they’re acting as a capitalist.

00:00:41.469 --> 00:00:43.890
And what’s their goal?

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:49.170
People will tell you it’s innovation, or
competition, but down in the real world it’s

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:51.809
pretty simple: capitalists have one goal.

00:00:51.809 --> 00:00:55.190
And that goal is to turn a profit.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:59.190
Pursuing profits to accumulate money is just
how capitalism works.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:00.680
That’s the nature of the beast.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:03.220
It’s the single impulse of capitalism.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:07.509
That doesn’t make capitalists personally
greedy, though some might be.

00:01:07.509 --> 00:01:12.830
We’re not even talking about good or evil
here: capitalists need to maximize profit,

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:17.910
to the exclusion of all other considerations,
or they’ll get eaten up by capitalists who

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.830
are smarter or more ruthless than them.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:23.520
That’s the jungle law of the market.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.940
So capitalists need profit to survive.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:27.910
But where does this profit come from?

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:29.610
That’s where you come in.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:30.710
Literally.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:31.960
Profit comes from YOU.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.100
Here’s a little thought experiment.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.660
Meet Harold.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.940
Harold has a chain of buildings full of kitchens
full of ingredients.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:42.180
But Harold doesn’t know how to make a burger
himself.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:47.030
How does Harold get someone to make enough
burgers that he can sell them and turn a profit?

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:49.880
It’s not a trick question – he pays you!

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.750
Because you know how to make really good burgers.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:56.369
The money he uses to pay you is what we call
“capital.”

00:01:56.369 --> 00:01:59.610
That’s money used to set production into
motion.

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:04.970
Let’s say Harold spent $1,000 buying all
the ingredients in the restaurant, and after

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:09.560
you work for a few weeks there, turning the
ingredients into burgers, they brought in

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:10.560
$3,000.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.170
That’s not bad!

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:16.840
You added $2,000 worth of value to the ingredients.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:17.850
But wait.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.670
You don’t get all the money.

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:23.220
Because Harold now has $3,000 on his hands.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:27.450
$1,000 of that is just making up for the cost
of the ingredients.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:31.920
And if you were paid for the full value of
your labor, you’d be making $2,000.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.070
But then Harold would only be breaking even
on the burgers.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:38.140
And he needs to make a profit in order to
survive.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:42.760
So Harold decides NOT to pay you for the full
value of your labor.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:46.010
Maybe he pays you just $1,000 of the value
you produced.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:48.120
Maybe he pays you $1,500.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:51.560
No matter what, you’ve been stolen from.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:55.540
You spent more of your labor than you were
compensated for.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:00.070
But here’s the dirty truth: the story doesn’t
end with you and Harold.

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:04.470
This process plays out across your city or
town, your state, the country and the entire

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:09.970
world – the rich get richer and the little
guy barely gets by.

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:15.500
We call the process - a boss’s stealing
from you - “exploitation.”

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:19.770
We don’t mean that in an emotional sense
about how we feel about it, but something

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:26.380
that’s actually a documented economic phenomenon
– the gap between how much the worker produces

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.380
and how much they get paid.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:33.150
Exploitation is a universal feature of capitalist
economies.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:39.400
And it never ends: the system requires more
and more exploitation – paying workers less,

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:44.380
making them work more, or making them more
productive without increasing wages.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:49.380
When you see in the newspaper that a corporation’s
recording record profits, that is what they

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:55.740
are doing: your hard work is producing more
value, but you’re not getting enough compensation

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:56.740
in return.

00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:59.770
There are tens of thousands of Harolds out
there.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:02.990
But billions of people just like you.

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:05.700
You and Harold are two different types of
people.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.190
You belong to two different classes.

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:13.370
There’s a capitalist who owns the means
to produce goods and services, and there’s

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:16.709
workers who only have their own labor to survive
on.

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:21.769
The capitalists appropriate the value that
the worker’s labor creates and keeps it

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:23.740
for themselves.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:25.440
And you are not immune from it.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.540
At any job you work at, the condition of your
employment is that you produce more by your

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.040
labor than you get paid.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:37.139
So in the capitalist system, no one is paid
what they’re worth.

00:04:37.139 --> 00:04:38.190
Capitalism means they get paid significantly
less.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.620
All profit is value extraction.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:46.620
And that means that all profit is theft -- from
you.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:50.830
This is Richard Wolff, professor of economics
at the New School and founder of Democracy

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:52.849
at Work, for the Gravel Institute.

